Maintainer: "Lizbeth Naranjo-Albarran" <lizbethna@ciencias.unam.mx>
Description: In many practical settings involving longitudinal data, the response variable is expected to follow specific shape constraints, such as monotonic trends -for example,
when patients' health is anticipated to decline over time. To address this, we propose a novel class of monotonic generalized additive models with mixed components
(mGAMMs) for longitudinal data. Within a Bayesian framework, monotonicity is
enforced through smoothing functions and penalized splines that capture increasing
or decreasing patterns, together with constraints on the prior distribution parameters. The approach is illustrated using both simulated data and a real health science
dataset, highlighting its computational advantages as well as potential limitations.
The proposed methods show improved fitting performance, particularly for small
datasets.
